(* tunnel-queries.pvl
   The queries of the tunnel models (AUD-5, #389; AUD-6, #409), numbered by their (* @query id *) comments as the queries
   of the session models are, and not conditional on a leak: a model has the compromise of its scenario or it has none.
   The id of every Tunnel is given to the attacker, so a query holds without the secrecy of the id: what keeps another
   node out of a Tunnel is the static key that the handshake authenticated, and what keeps a middlebox out is the binding
   of the handshake to its outer session. The server knows a joining connection by its static key, so the events of the
   server name the key.
*)

(* A connection of alice can join, so that no query below is true only because nothing happens *)
(* @query reach-join *)
query t: bitstring, id: bitstring; event(ServerJoined(bob, pk(skey(alice)), id, t)).

(* A connection with the static key of alice that joins a Tunnel is a connection that alice made, in the outer session
   that the server is in. A middlebox that terminates the outer session of alice and opens one to the server is the case
   that the binding rules out. *)
(* @query join-auth *)
query t: bitstring, id: bitstring; event(ServerJoined(bob, pk(skey(alice)), id, t)) ==> event(ClientJoin(alice, bob, id, t)).

(* A connection joins only a Tunnel that was made for its own static key, whatever ids it knows: alice does not join a
   Tunnel of charlie, and charlie, whose keys the attacker holds, does not join a Tunnel of alice *)
(* @query join-identity *)
query t: bitstring, id: bitstring, k: bitstring; event(ServerJoined(bob, k, id, t)) ==> event(TunnelOpened(bob, k, id)).

(* No replay: each join that the server takes is of a Login that was sent once *)
(* @query join-replay *)
query t: bitstring, id: bitstring; inj-event(ServerJoined(bob, pk(skey(alice)), id, t)) ==> inj-event(ClientJoin(alice, bob, id, t)).
